### Q1 — The repair: right masks, right form, right member?

* **(a) Premises (`S1-S16`, `P1-P6`, `P9-P10`):**
  * **`S6` and `P5` are BROKEN on `init_block_addr` (`DERIVED` from `3.2:1-3`, `5:16-19`, `6:9-11`, `8.1:2-3`):** `S6` (`5:18-19`) and `P5` (`6:9-11`) claim that CSR1's bit 0 feeds *only* the parity warning and that read-back is the fix's *only* consequence. Look at `le_chip_init()` in `3.2:1-2` and `8.1:2`:
    `init_block_addr = (d->reg[1] & 0xffff) + ((d->reg[2] & 0xff) << 16)`
    Because `d->reg[1]` is masked with `0xffff` (not `0xfffe`) when forming `init_block_addr`, under HEAD a guest that writes an odd value to CSR1 and sets `CSR0.INIT` causes `le_chip_init()` not only to print `"WARNING! address not word aligned?"` (`8.1:3`), but also to read the 24-byte Initialization Block from an **odd DMA address** (`init_block_addr | 1`). Under M1 (`d->reg[1] = x & 0xfffe`), `init_block_addr` has bit 0 cleared (`init_block_addr & ~1`), aligning the DMA read to a 16-bit word boundary. That is a second functional consequence of M1 beyond RDP read-back and the warning.
  * **`S1-S5`, `S7-S8`, `P6` (`READ` at `8.2:839-888`; callers/readers `UNSETTLED` / `NOT CHECKABLE`, requires `src/devices/dev_le.c`, `src/include/if_lereg.h`, `src/include/dec_kn01.h`, `src/machines/machine_pmax.c`):** Inside `8.2:839-888`, `le_register_write(struct le_data *d, int r, uint32_t x)` is non-static (`8.2:839`), handles `case 0:` (`8.2:842-882`), and sends every non-zero `int r` to `default:` (`8.2:884-886`). Because `8.1` and `8.3-8.5` are omitted (`8.1:1`, `8.3:1`), verifying that `DEVICE_ACCESS(le)` at `dev_le.c:1002` is the sole caller, that RAP masks `d->reg_select` by `idata & (N_REGISTERS - 1)` at `dev_le.c:1015` ( confining `r` to `{0, 1, 2, 3}` so `default:` after `case 1:` and `case 2:` is exclusively `r == 3`, `P6`), and that `d->reg[3]` has no readers (`P5`) requires `src/devices/dev_le.c`. The witness run empirically confirms the reset signature `0x0004` (`2.2:24`) and RAP mask `0x13 -> 0x0003` (`2.2:25`).
  * **`S9-S16` (`READ` / `DERIVED` from `2.1:1-9`, `2.2:1-40`, `2.3:1-16`, `4:1-10`, `5:20-48`, `9.1:1121-1226`):** Hold as stated in the packet (files `regress/gate_hygiene.sh`, `regress/gate_arm.sh`, and `tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py` are not inlined in full, so their line numbers in `S14-S16` are `NOT CHECKABLE` outside the quotes).
  * **`P1` and `P2` (`DERIVED` architectural mask `0xfffe`; physical Am7990 silicon read-back of CSR1 bit 0 is `UNSETTLED` / `NOT CHECKABLE`, requires a readable Am7990 datasheet because `_scratchpad/lance/am7990_ds.txt` is an unreadable scan per `6:5`):** In `9.1:1130` and `9.1:1138-1142` (Am79C90) and `9.2:530-532` (Am7990 Technical Manual Figure 1-3), CSR1 is partitioned into `15:01 IADR` and bit `00` drawn as `` `0' `` (`9.1:1130`) / `00` (`9.2:531`) with description `"Must be zero."` (`9.1:1142`). Unlike CSR2 bits 15:8 (`9.1:1161-1163`) and CSR3 bits 15:3 (`9.1:1222-1223`), CSR1 bit 0 is not labeled `RES Reserved. Read as zeroes. Write as zeroes.` because conceptually it is address bit `IADR[0]` of a word-aligned pointer rather than an unallocated field. Neither text in `9.1` or `9.2` explicitly states whether a host that violates `"Must be zero"` reads back `0` or `1` on bit 0, so `P1`/`P2` cannot be settled from prose alone, though drawing hardwired `'0'` / `00` in both diagrams and `rpajarola` commit `e108b54` (`1.2:1-4`) support `0xfffe`.
  * **`P3` and `P4` (`READ` at `9.1:1161-1163`, `9.1:1222-1226`, `9.2:534-540`):** Hold. CSR2 bits `15:08` are `RES` (`"Reserved. Read as zeroes. Write as zeroes."`) and `07:00` are `IADR[23:16]` -> mask `0x00ff` (`P3`). CSR3 bits `15:03` are `RES` (`"Reserved. Read as zeroes. Write as zeroes."`) and `02:00` are `BSWP`, `ACON`, `BCON` -> mask `0x0007` (`P4`).
